[ask_8db1fabf] done in 308.2s; estimate 316,030,734,102,294,400; usd 0.7857
Best computed estimate for the pinned target — total tokens processed worldwide by all large language models during calendar year 2025, across API and first-party products, input plus output including cached and reasoning tokens — is 316,030,734,102,294,400 token (80% model-based interval 93,645,241,551,696,832 to 1,066,529,630,788,542,720 token). This comes from method m3 at weight 100%, an energy route: IEA's AI-focused data-centre electricity for 2025 of 155 TWh, multiplied by an assumed inference share of 0.7 (range 0.461 to 0.922) and an assumed 3,000 tokens per Wh (range 1,000 to 10,000). The electricity figure is observed; the two conversion factors are analyst assumptions, not measurements. Other methods (provider-disclosure bottom-up, Google-only, OpenRouter-scaled, third-party annualized) were discarded for missing inputs, so no cross-method averaging occurred. The result is most fragile to the tokens-per-Wh assumption, which spans an order of magnitude and varies with model size, batch and utilisation. Better evidence would be provider-reported annual token volumes and measured inference energy per token.

Key assumptions (proposed by the analyst model, not observed; code samples each one between the bounds shown):
- a3 Inference share of AI-focused data-centre electricity, 2025: 0.7 share, range 0.461 to 0.922 (range widened by code); used by m3. Overturned by: Evidence that training dominates AI DC electricity in 2025.
- a4 Tokens per Wh of inference energy, 2025 frontier serving: 3,000 tokens per Wh, range 1,000 to 10,000; used by m3. Overturned by: A measured 2025 tokens-per-joule benchmark outside [n]–[n] tokens/Wh.
